{#- console-header.njk — ONE header for both consoles

   There were two, and they drifted: the staff one grew a link to the admin
   area, the admin one grew a way back, and neither could describe somebody who
   belonged in both. This renders BOTH rows and lets the account decide which
   it gets — one row for most people, two for somebody who is genuinely both.

   EVERY LINK IS RENDERED AND THEN HIDDEN, rather than rendered conditionally.
   The pages are static: Eleventy does not know who is asking, and building a
   variant per role would mean shipping the whole console several times over.
   So the roles ride along in `data-roles` and staff.js removes what does not
   apply, from an identity it already has cached before first paint.

   WHICH MEANS THIS IS NOT A SECURITY BOUNDARY, and nothing here should ever be
   mistaken for one. Every endpoint checks the role itself. A hidden link is a
   tidier console, not a closed door. -#}

<header class="console">
  <div class="console-in">

    {#- The wordmark is a link to whichever console the person actually has.
       It used to be hardcoded to /staff/, which sent a board member to a page
       that refused them the moment they clicked their own logo. -#}
    <a class="mark console-mark" id="consoleHome" href="/staff/">THAUMA</a>

    <div class="console-rows">
      {%- for area in ["staff", "admin"] %}
      {%- set list = consoleNav.staff if area == "staff" else consoleNav.admin %}
      {%- set current = staffPage if area == "staff" else adminPage %}
      {#- The roles are BOTH a class and an attribute. The class is what CSS
         matches, so the right rows are there in the first paint; the attribute
         is what staff.js reads afterwards. Same list, two readers. -#}
      <div class="console-row r-{{ consoleNav.rows[area] | join(' r-') }}"
           data-row="{{ area }}"
           data-roles="{{ consoleNav.rows[area] | join(' ') }}">
        <span class="console-badge is-{{ area }}"
              data-i18n="nav.badge.{{ area }}">{{ "Staff" if area == "staff" else "Administration" }}</span>
        <nav>
          {%- for p in list %}
          <a href="{{ p.url }}" class="r-{{ p.roles | join(' r-') }}"
             data-roles="{{ p.roles | join(' ') }}"
             {% if p.slug == current %}aria-current="page"{% endif %}
             data-i18n="{{ ('nav.' + ('dashboard' if p.slug == 'index' else p.slug)) if area == 'staff' else ('adm.nav.' + p.slug) }}">{{ p.label }}</a>
          {%- endfor %}
        </nav>
      </div>
      {%- endfor %}
    </div>

    {#- Vertically centred beside however many rows there are, rather than
       living inside the first one. Putting it in a row meant the actions moved
       down the header when a second row appeared, and moved back when it did
       not. -#}
    <div class="console-actions">
      <span class="pill" id="partnerPill" hidden></span>
      <a class="area-switch" href="/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        <span data-i18n="nav.viewSite">Public site</span>
        <span class="area-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&#8599;</span>
      </a>
      <a class="ghost-btn" href="/cdn-cgi/access/logout" data-i18n="nav.signout">Sign out</a>
    </div>

  </div>
</header>
